                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the turn before you cost the robots`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Resist the instinct to cost £60M of automation in eight minutes. Meridian is two generations behind on tech — but its <strong>only cultural asset is 11 years of workforce loyalty</strong>, and engagement sits at 41% with just 23% of staff able to say where the company is going. White's EY-Oxford 2024 study found the <em>human factors, not the technology</em>, decide whether a transformation lands. Classify what kind of turn this really is, locate the load-bearing problem, and name the single hardest stakeholder you must <em>convert</em>, not merely manage. The trap: optimising the 30% that rarely fails while ignoring who will actually run the robots — and whether they believe in the turn.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Frame the “why” before the “what”`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `A plan announced before a shared <strong>why</strong> produces compliance, not commitment. Craft the spine of the change story in three beats — purpose, honest threat, and the workforce's role in it — then decide your <strong>first move</strong> and the order it lands. The board wants margin and pace; the union wants security and a voice; the depot floor wants to know if their work still matters. The same facts, three different questions — one generic message answers none of them. And the redundancy question is hanging in the air: decide what you cannot yet say, and how to equip the frontline managers — the most-trusted voices — to retell it. Beware the <em>burning platform</em> that frightens the very people you need to stay and build.`,
