                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_survival:      `<strong>Right framing.</strong> Lose the anchor without visible progress and the grocer follows — this is existential. But you named it survival without forgetting the only asset you have is the people. That tension is the whole job.`,
                    coach_r1_efficiency:    `<strong>An efficiency lens narrows you too fast.</strong> Squeeze-and-automate hits the grocer's cost-down but treats the workforce as a cost line, not the engine of the turn. You'll get the kit and lose the people who run it.`,
                    coach_r1_growth:        `<strong>A pure growth play is a luxury you don't have yet.</strong> The anchor is under review and the share price fell 18%. Chase new contracts before you've stabilised the two that are 46% of revenue and you may have neither.`,
                    coach_r1_bauplus:       `<strong>That's exactly the plan the last board deferred for six years.</strong> Funding the deferred robotics upgrade as business-as-usual is the technology-first reflex — it ignores that engagement is 41% and the turn is a leadership act, not a procurement.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named it a survival turn AND located the load-bearing problem in leadership and culture — exactly where White's EY-Oxford evidence says transformations are won or lost. Now everything you do has to honour that.`,
                    coach_r1_techfirst:     `<strong>You've costed the robotics before diagnosing the culture.</strong> You're optimising the 30% that rarely fails. Engagement is 41% and only 23% know where the company is going. Who is going to run these robots, and do they believe in the turn?`,
                    coach_r1_costfirst:     `<strong>A hard restructuring is the fastest way to destroy your only asset.</strong> The 11-year tenure loyalty is what lets you execute the next two years. Lead with cost-cutting and you'll buy a quarter of margin and lose the capacity to deliver anything.`,
                    coach_r1_strategy:      `<strong>Strategy isn't the binding constraint here.</strong> The markets and clients are clear — the question is whether 5,800 people will follow you through the change. That's a leadership-and-culture problem, not a portfolio one.`,
                    coach_r1_wrongstake:    `<strong>Managing the board or the analysts isn't the same as leading the turn.</strong> The hardest stakeholder you must <em>convert</em> is the unionised frontline — without their belief the robots don't get run and the plan doesn't execute, whatever the City thinks.`,
                    coach_r1_shallow:       `<strong>You've skipped the diagnosis.</strong> Costing a plan on a shallow read of the human factors is how the previous leadership deferred this for six years. Go deeper on why engagement is 41% before you commit a pound.`,
                    coach_r2_purpose:       `<strong>Purpose before plan — that's the order that earns commitment.</strong> Anchoring the why in purpose, an honest threat, and the workforce's role gives a forklift operator a reason to stay and build, not just comply.`,
                    coach_r2_burning:       `<strong>You've just told 5,800 people the building is on fire.</strong> Engagement was already 41%; half will polish their CVs and the best leave first. Fear creates urgency and paralysis at the same time. Where in your message is the reason to stay?`,
                    coach_r2_vision:        `<strong>An inspiring vision with no honest threat feels like spin to a sceptical floor.</strong> They know the anchor is under review. Name the threat honestly alongside the purpose, or the vision reads as the same hype the last leadership sold.`,
                    coach_r2_numbers:       `<strong>Walking the floor through EBIT and the £60M plan is the “what,” not the “why.”</strong> A plan before a shared why produces compliance. The numbers reassure the board; they don't mobilise a forklift operator at 5am.`,
                    coach_r2_boardfirst:    `<strong>Locking the board mandate first buys authority but spends trust.</strong> If the floor hears the plan from a leaked board pack rather than from you, the “done to us, not with us” story writes itself. Sequence the people in.`,
                    coach_r2_clientfirst:   `<strong>A client pledge before you've spoken to your own people is a hostage to fortune.</strong> You're promising the anchor what your workforce hasn't yet bought into — exactly how over-promises are born.`,
                    coach_r2_tailored:      `<strong>One coherent why, three audiences — that's the craft.</strong> Same facts; the board hears margin and pace, the union hears security and voice, the floor hears that their work still matters. That answers all three questions instead of none.`,
                    coach_r2_onedeck:       `<strong>One deck for the board, the union, and the floor answers none of them.</strong> The board wants margin, the union wants jobs, the floor wants meaning — the same slides leave all three cold. Tailor the telling without splitting the why.`,
                    coach_r2_boardonly:     `<strong>Speaking mainly to the City leaves the floor to fill the silence with rumour.</strong> The people who run the depots are the ones who decide whether the turn happens. They can't follow a why they never heard.`,
                    coach_r2_deny:          `<strong>“No redundancies” is a promise you cannot keep.</strong> It buys calm now and detonates in Round 4 when the memo leaks. The fastest way to lose a workforce's trust is to be caught having lied to protect it.`,
                    coach_r2_silent:        `<strong>Saying nothing about jobs doesn't make the question go away.</strong> Silence on the one thing everyone is thinking about reads as evasion. Name the tension honestly and commit to redeploy-first while you still control the narrative.`,
                    coach_r3_phased:        `<strong>Pilot-and-scale respects the capacity to absorb change.</strong> One depot first lets you learn, prove a deliverable win, and adapt before scaling — momentum that survives the inevitable mid-course slump.`,
                    coach_r3_bigbang:       `<strong>Big-bang on both depots satisfies the board's appetite for speed and ignores the floor's capacity.</strong> Transform faster than people can absorb and you trade trust for a timeline — and you have no pilot to learn from when it slips.`,
                    coach_r3_humancentered: `<strong>This is human-centered pacing.</strong> Pilot-and-scale with redeploy-and-reskill keeps the 11-year loyalty intact while you build — slower, but it preserves the capacity to actually execute the next two years.`,
                    coach_r3_voluntary:     `<strong>Voluntary-only is a defensible middle.</strong> You avoid forced cuts and most of the union risk, but you'll lose some experienced people you'd have kept, and the redeployment story is half-told. Watch the capacity you're giving up.`,
                    coach_r3_compulsory:    `<strong>You've cut 900 roles to hit the board's timeline.</strong> That's ~£9M, a near-certain strike at 62% density, and you've destroyed the 11-year tenure loyalty that was your only cultural asset. You bought four months of speed — at the cost of the capacity to execute the next two years.`,
                    coach_r3_underinvest:   `<strong>Under-investing won't move the anchor.</strong> The fashion client put you under review because you can't offer automated, data-integrated fulfilment. A token spend leaves the very capability gap that started this — and the renewal is in months.`,
                    coach_r3_overinvest:    `<strong>Committing too much too fast strains £22M of free cash flow.</strong> The £60M is over three years against £31M EBIT. Front-load it all and you spook the board and the analysts you're trying to reassure. Pace the spend, not just the people.`,
                    coach_r3_overpromise:   `<strong>You've pledged a bold, client-visible win.</strong> If it lands, you buy credibility — but Round 4 is the trough, and an over-promise you miss is far more expensive than a smaller one you over-deliver. Credibility is spent once.`,
                    coach_r3_timid:         `<strong>Too timid on early wins and the anchor sees no proof.</strong> The whole point of momentum is a visible, credible signal before month 11. No early win and the client's review hardens into a decision to leave.`,
                    coach_r3_nocapacity:    `<strong>You've mandated change with no protected capacity.</strong> Telling a loyal but stretched workforce to reskill and re-tool with no backfill produces burnout and resistance, not adoption. Protect the capacity you're asking them to spend.`,
                    coach_r4_hold:          `<strong>Holding pace and leading through it is the leadership act.</strong> You owned the slip, fixed the cohort, and kept your human-centered commitments. The trough is exactly where the turn is won or lost — and you stayed in it.`,
                    coach_r4_slow:          `<strong>Slowing down protects the people but spends time you don't have.</strong> A later anchor milestone is a real cost with the renewal at month 11. Defensible if you've banked an early win — dangerous if you haven't.`,
                    coach_r4_double:        `<strong>Doubling down on speed at the trough breaks the very people you need.</strong> Pushing harder when a cohort is already struggling and a memo has leaked tells the floor the human-centered language was always just words.`,
                    coach_r4_front:         `<strong>Fronting the leak honestly — floor first — is leading through it, not around it.</strong> You spent Round 2 building a narrative about trusting the workforce with the truth. Confirming that under pressure is what makes the narrative real.`,
                    coach_r4_holding:       `<strong>A corporate holding statement is leading around the trough, not through it.</strong> Staying above it confirms the floor's worst reading: that the “with you, not to you” language was always PR. The moment calls for the CEO, not the comms team.`,
                    coach_r4_silent:        `<strong>Silence now confirms the workforce's worst reading of you.</strong> The memo is on the union's desk and in the press. You spent Round 2 promising honesty; going quiet at the trough spends every ounce of trust you built.`,
                    coach_r4_spin:          `<strong>Burying the slip to project confidence is a one-time trick.</strong> The board and the anchor find out anyway, and then you're the leader who said “trust me” and span. Honesty on the slip is what makes the destination credible.`,
                    coach_r4_overpromise:   `<strong>Re-promising the original timeline to keep the anchor calm doubles the bet you're already losing.</strong> You missed once; promising the same date again is how a credibility problem becomes a credibility collapse.`,
                    coach_r4_promisebites:  `<strong>The bold pledge from Round 3 has come due — and slipped.</strong> You're now the leader who said “trust me” and missed, and you didn't front it honestly. A smaller promise you could over-deliver would have been worth far more than this.`,

